            "solutionSteps": [
                "Splitting the middle term: product = -36, sum = 5.",
                "Factors are +9 and -4.",
                "$6x^2 + 9x - 4x - 6$.",
                "$3x(2x+3) - 2(2x+3)$.",
                "$(2x+3)(3x-2)$."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q8",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 3,
            "question": "If $x + y + z = 0$, show that $x^3 + y^3 + z^3 = 3xyz$.",
            "finalAnswer": "Proof",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "Identity: $x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz = (x+y+z)(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx)$.",
                "Since $x+y+z=0$, the RHS becomes 0.",
                "$x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz = 0 \\implies x^3+y^3+z^3 = 3xyz$."
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "q9",
            "type": "subjective",
            "marks": 5,
            "question": "Verify that $x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 3xyz = \\frac{1}{2}(x+y+z)[(x-y)^2 + (y-z)^2 + (z-x)^2]$.",
            "finalAnswer": "Verified",
            "solutionSteps": [
                "RHS = $\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+z)(x^2-2xy+y^2 + y^2-2yz+z^2 + z^2-2zx+x^2)$.",
                "$= \\frac{1}{2}(x+y+z)(2x^2+2y^2+2z^2-2xy-2yz-2zx)$.",
                "$= (x+y+z)(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx)$.",
                "This matches the standard expansion of $x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz$."
            ]
        },
